    {
      "name": "9. Auth — the login flow (browser-driven)",
      "description": "The OAuth2 + PKCE authorization-code flow. READ THE FOLDER DESCRIPTION IN REQUEST 1 BEFORE RUNNING: the redirect target is currently an in-cluster URL, so this flow cannot be completed from outside the cluster.",
      "item": [
        {
          "name": "1. Start login — GET /bff/login",
          "request": {
            "method": "GET",
            "url": {
              "raw": "{{bffUrl}}/bff/login",
              "host": [
                "{{bffUrl}}"
              ],
              "path": [
                "bff",
                "login"
              ]
            },
            "description": "Verified live 2026-09-14. Returns 302 with:\n\n  Set-Cookie: __Host-zonic_login=<id>; HttpOnly; Max-Age=600; Path=/; SameSite=lax; Secure\n  Location:   http://zonic-authorization-server.zonic-authz.svc.cluster.local/oauth2/authorize?client_id=zonic-bff&redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fauth.dev.tripleq.ai%2Fbff%2Fcallback&response_type=code&code_challenge=<S256>&code_challenge_method=S256&state=<opaque>\n\n=== THIS FLOW CANNOT BE COMPLETED FROM POSTMAN TODAY ===\n\nThe Location is the Authorization Server's IN-CLUSTER Service URL. It resolves only inside the EKS cluster, so neither Postman nor a browser can follow it. That is not a misconfiguration to work around here: the Authorization Server has NO Ingress at all (its Helm chart has no ingress template), and ZONIC_AUTHZ_ISSUER is deliberately set to the Service URL because that is the only address it has.\n\nTurn OFF Postman's 'Automatically follow redirects' (Settings → General) so you can read the 302 rather than watch it fail.\n\nWhat you CAN verify here, and it is worth doing:\n  * the 302 is issued at all (the BFF composed and reached its stores)\n  * __Host-zonic_login is set, Secure + HttpOnly, 600s\n  * code_challenge_method=S256 and a fresh code_challenge per call\n  * state is a fresh opaque value per call\n  * client_id=zonic-bff and redirect_uri points back at this BFF\n\nCall it twice: code_challenge and state MUST differ. If they repeat, the PKCE generator is broken and the flow is replayable.\n\nTo make this reachable, the Authorization Server needs an internal Ingress and a public issuer, and ZONIC_AUTHZ_ISSUER must match it exactly — the issuer is a signed token claim, so changing it invalidates tokens minted under the old value."
          },
          "event": [
            {
              "listen": "test",
              "script": {
                "type": "text/javascript",
                "exec": [
                  "// Read the 302 without following it.",
                  "pm.test('302 redirect issued', function () {",
                  "    pm.expect(pm.response.code).to.eql(302);",
                  "});",
                  "const loc = pm.response.headers.get('Location') || '';",
                  "pm.test('PKCE uses S256', function () {",
                  "    pm.expect(loc).to.include('code_challenge_method=S256');",
                  "});",
                  "pm.test('client_id is zonic-bff', function () {",
                  "    pm.expect(loc).to.include('client_id=zonic-bff');",
                  "});",
                  "// Keep state so a second run can prove it changes.",
                  "const m = loc.match(/[?&]state=([^&]+)/);",
                  "if (m) {",
                  "    const prev = pm.collectionVariables.get('lastLoginState');",
                  "    if (prev) {",
                  "        pm.test('state differs from the previous login', function () {",
                  "            pm.expect(m[1]).to.not.eql(prev);",
                  "        });",
                  "    }",
                  "    pm.collectionVariables.set('lastLoginState', m[1]);",
                  "}"
                ]
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "2. Callback — GET /bff/callback (reference only)",
          "request": {
            "method": "GET",
            "url": {
              "raw": "{{bffUrl}}/bff/callback?code={{authCode}}&state={{authState}}",
              "host": [
                "{{bffUrl}}"
              ],
              "path": [
                "bff",
                "callback"
              ],
              "query": [
                {
                  "key": "code",
                  "value": "{{authCode}}"
                },
                {
                  "key": "state",
                  "value": "{{authState}}"
                }
              ]
            },
            "description": "WHEN THIS FIRES: the Authorization Server redirects the BROWSER here after the user submits credentials at its /login page. You never call it by hand in a working flow — it is the browser's second hop, carrying ?code= and ?state= as query parameters.\n\nWhat it does, in order (src/zonic_bff/api/routes.py):\n  1. reads __Host-zonic_login from the request cookies; 400 'no login in progress for this browser' if absent\n  2. CONSUMES the login state — single use. A replay of the same callback gets 400 'login state expired or already used'\n  3. compares state exactly (RFC 6749 s10.12); a mismatch means the request did not originate from the redirect this BFF issued\n  4. exchanges the code + PKCE verifier for tokens at the Authorization Server\n  5. sets __Host-zonic_session (HttpOnly) and __Host-zonic_csrf (NOT HttpOnly, so the frontend can read it), deletes the login cookie, and 302s to the post-login redirect\n\nCalling this in Postman without a real code returns 400. That is correct behaviour, not a broken request — and the two 400s above are worth triggering deliberately, because they are the CSRF and replay defences."
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "3. Get an access token — GET /bff/session/token",
          "request": {
            "method": "GET",
            "url": {
              "raw": "{{bffUrl}}/bff/session/token",
              "host": [
                "{{bffUrl}}"
              ],
              "path": [
                "bff",
                "session",
                "token"
              ]
            },
            "description": "Verified live 2026-09-14 WITHOUT a session cookie:\n\n    401 {\"detail\":\"no session cookie\"}\n\nThat is the correct unauthenticated response and the one you can reproduce today. With a valid __Host-zonic_session cookie it returns:\n\n    200 {\"access_token\":\"<JWT>\",\"expires_in\":900}\n\nThe access token is NEVER stored in the browser — the frontend calls this endpoint each time it needs one, and the refresh token stays server-side, encrypted, in Redis. This is the entire point of the BFF pattern: a stolen XSS payload cannot exfiltrate a refresh token it can never see.\n\nIf the access token has expired, the BFF refreshes it transparently (refresher.ensure_fresh). A 401 'session could not be refreshed' means the refresh token itself expired — 30 days — and the user must log in again."
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "4. Logout — POST /bff/session/logout",
          "request": {
            "method": "POST",
            "url": {
              "raw": "{{bffUrl}}/bff/session/logout",
              "host": [
                "{{bffUrl}}"
              ],
              "path": [
                "bff",
                "session",
                "logout"
              ]
            },
            "description": "Requires BOTH:\n  * the __Host-zonic_session cookie (sent automatically by a browser)\n  * an X-Zonic-CSRF-Token header whose value equals the __Host-zonic_csrf cookie\n\nThis is the double-submit CSRF defence. A cross-site form can make the browser SEND the session cookie, but cannot make it set a custom header — so the header is the proof the request came from your own frontend. The comparison is constant-time (hmac.compare_digest).\n\nWithout the header: 403 {\"detail\":\"CSRF validation failed\"}.\nWithout a session cookie: 401 — the session dependency runs first.\n\nThe CSRF cookie is deliberately NOT HttpOnly: the frontend reads it from document.cookie to set this header. That is the supported pattern, not a leak.\n\nOn success the BFF revokes the refresh token at the Authorization Server, deletes the server-side session, and clears both cookies.",
            "header": [
              {
                "key": "X-Zonic-CSRF-Token",
                "value": "{{csrfToken}}",
                "description": "Must equal the __Host-zonic_csrf cookie value."
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
